:  \n\n\\[\n23 = 20 + 3\n\\]\n\nThen apply the distributive law \\(a(b+c)=ab+ac\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n17 \\times 23 \n&= 17 \\times (20 + 3) \\\\\n&= (17 \\times 20) + (17 \\times 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow compute each product:\n\n* \\(17 \\times 20 = 340\\) (because \\(17 \\times 2 = 34\\), then add a zero)\n* \\(17 \\times 3 = 51\\)\n\nAdd the two results:\n\n\\[\n340 + 51 = 391\n\\]\n\n---\n\n## Approach 2: Standard multiplication algorithm  \n\nSet up the multiplication vertically and multiply by each digit of the second number.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\ \\ \\ 17\\\\\n\\times 23\\\\ \\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n1. **Multiply by the ones digit (3):** \\(3 \\times 7 = 21\\) → write down **1**, carry **2**.  \n   \\(3 \\times 1 = 3\\); add the carry: \\(3 + 2 = 5\\).  \n   So the first partial product is **51**.\n\n2. **Multiply by the tens digit (2):** because it’s in the tens place, we first place a zero as a placeholder.\n\n   - \\(2 \\times 7 = 14\\) → write down **4**, carry **1**.  \n   - \\(2 \\times 1 = 2\\); add the carry: \\(2 + 1 = 3\\).  \n   So the second partial product is **340** (the zero is already placed).\n\n3. **Add the partial products:**  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\ \\ \\ 51\\\\\n+340\\\\ \\hline\n\\ \\ 391\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{17 \\times 23 = 391}\n\\]"}],"role":"assistant"}},"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"inputTokens":56,"outputTokens":914,"serverToolUsage":{},"totalTokens":970}}